  "signature": "(-,+)",
  "convention": "We keep factors of $c$ explicit. The metric and the dilaton $\\phi$ solve $R = -2/L^2$ and $\\nabla_\\mu\\nabla_\\nu\\phi - g_{\\mu\\nu}\\nabla^2\\phi + g_{\\mu\\nu}\\phi/L^2 = 0$, with $\\phi_r$ a length that sets the strength of the dilaton. The Ricci tensor is the standard contraction, $R_{\\mu\\nu} = R^\\alpha{}_{\\mu\\alpha\\nu}$.",
  "history": "In two dimensions Einstein's equation is empty [mertens2023]. The Einstein tensor of every metric with one dimension of space and one of time vanishes identically, so matter coupled to it is forced to have a vanishing stress tensor, and no energy can flow [mertens2023]. Claudio Teitelboim's way out, published in 1983, rested on something two dimensions allow, a central charge in the algebra of deformations of a surface, with its inverse \"playing the role of the gravitational constant\" [teitelboim1983]. Roman Jackiw proposed \"a constant curvature model for two dimensional space-time, analogous to the theory in three dimensional space-time\" [jackiw1985]. In the form that carries their two names, a scalar field $\\phi$, the dilaton, multiplies $R + 2/L^2$ in the action, and its equation holds the curvature at $R = -2/L^2$ everywhere [mertens2023, maldacena2016].¶Every solution is then a piece of one surface, anti-de Sitter space of two dimensions, and only the dilaton tells one solution from another [mertens2023]. The analog of the black hole came from J. David Brown, Marc Henneaux, and Teitelboim in 1986, a metric of constant curvature \"everywhere except on a singular line\", with an event horizon [brown1986black]. It also radiates, with \"an analog of Hawking radiation with temperature proportional to the strength (mass) of the singularity\" [brown1986black]. José P. S. Lemos and Paulo M. Sá wrote down a hole with no singular line, $ds^2 = -(a^2r^2 - 1)dt^2 + dr^2/(a^2r^2 - 1)$ with the dilaton proportional to $r$, the static chart at $r_h = L$ with $a = 1/L$, and \"free of spacetime singularities\" [lemos1994]. Past $r = 0$ its metric runs on with \"no singularity, no horizon, no infinity\", and they followed it into \"an infinite chain of universes connected by timelike wormholes\" [lemos1994].¶Why cut a smooth spacetime off where a scalar field vanishes? Ana Achúcarro and Miguel E. Ortiz answered from three dimensions [achucarro1993]. They wrote the black hole of Bañados, Teitelboim, and Zanelli without rotation as a metric of two dimensions times a circle of radius $\\phi$, and Einstein's action with a negative cosmological constant became Jackiw and Teitelboim's [achucarro1993]. The hole itself became $ds^2 = -(\\Lambda r^2 - M)dt^2 + dr^2/(\\Lambda r^2 - M)$ with $\\phi = r$, and since the hole of three dimensions is singular where the circle closes, they cut the plane off at $\\phi = 0$, though its metric is \"perfectly well-behaved\" there [achucarro1993]. Read the same way, the dilaton is the size of what was integrated out, and its value on the horizon is the entropy, $S = (\\phi_0 + \\phi_h)/4G$ with $\\phi_0$ a constant added to the dilaton, the analog of Bekenstein and Hawking's area over four [mertens2023]. Since $\\phi_h = \\phi_rr_h/L^2$ and the temperature is $T = \\hbar c\\,r_h/2\\pi k_BL^2$, the entropy grows in proportion to the temperature [mertens2023, maldacena2016].¶A charged black hole near extremality grows a long throat, and the throat obeys this gravity [mertens2023]. With the charge $Q$ of a Reissner-Nordström hole held fixed and its mass brought down toward $Q$, the region near the horizon, in units of $Q$, is $-(\\tilde r^2 - r_h^2)dt^2 + d\\tilde r^2/(\\tilde r^2 - r_h^2)$, the static chart at $L = 1$, times a sphere whose area plays the dilaton [mertens2023]. Ahmed Almheiri and Joseph Polchinski built models of gravity with a dilaton in two dimensions that flow to anti-de Sitter space, to study backreaction, the pull of matter on the surrounding geometry [almheiri2015]. Juan Maldacena, Douglas Stanford, and Zhenbin Yang took the leading part of such a model to be the gravity of Jackiw and Teitelboim, which \"describes near extremal black holes, or more generally, nearly $AdS_2$ spacetimes\" [maldacena2016]. Its gravitational corrections set the growth of correlators \"indicative of chaos\" [maldacena2016].¶The quantum theory can be solved exactly [mertens2023]. In Euclidean signature the black hole is a disc of the hyperbolic plane, the horizon at its centre and the period of its angle set by the temperature, and its whole gravity reduces to the wiggles of the disc's boundary, governed by a Schwarzian action [mertens2023, maldacena2016]. Thomas G. Mertens and Gustavo J. Turiaci call the degree of solvability \"unprecedented\", and the corrections from surfaces with handles, which they call wormholes, can be included as well [mertens2023]. Ahmed Almheiri, Raghu Mahajan, Juan Maldacena, and Ying Zhao let a black hole of this gravity radiate into a bath and computed the entropy of its Hawking radiation, which rises and then falls as unitarity requires, the Page curve [almheiri2020]. The fall comes from \"islands\", regions of the black hole's interior that belong to the radiation after the Page time [almheiri2020].",
